## Resource guard, browsers and processes

Run compilers, tests, lint, installs, image generation and browsers through
`python3 tools/heavy.py run --kind KIND --wait 3600 -- COMMAND`. Submit once;
do not poll status or retry exit 75 in a loop. It means policy or resources
refused the job. Only the coordinator grants high priority. One heavy command
per worker at a time. For detached Paseo work use `HEAVY_NOTIFY=auto` on the
outer guard, never exported and never inside Makefile recipes, then end the
turn. The guard's event reports completion, including failure. Nested commands
reuse their parent's slot. Read mode and memory policy in `tools/README.md`.

Browser work holds the exclusive browser lock. Use only Playwright Chromium or
Chrome for Testing, headless with a unique temporary profile, closed in finally
and on process exit. Never drive the owner's everyday browser or attach its
profile. Use `tools/chrome/` for screenshots and DOM probes. Do not trust a
successful bounds probe as evidence that a screen looks good: inspect images.

Start interactive test sessions and disposable services with an explicit minimal
environment allowlist and only the test credentials they need. Never print or
return an environment, runtime config or credential-bearing object in a REPL;
suppress expression return values when constructing such objects.

Stop only your own processes, by verified PID. Never broad-match a process
name, `pgrep -f` variable or `killall`. Killing a parent may leave children;
check the owned profile/port after cleanup. Preserve owner-managed tmux servers.
Do not start unattended dev servers in worker worktrees. Tests use controlled
test builds and independent ports. When developing on a local machine use
`python3 self_host.py dev-start` instead of `start`, unless told otherwise.

Use real load-bearing binaries (`command ...` in shell code). Quote literal
paths and patterns. On macOS assume BSD userland unless calling GNU tools by
their g-prefixed name. Read `CODEX_BIN` rather than invoking a shell wrapper.
Pin the Go toolchain in the Makefile; the hosting script reads that exported pin. Pass optional proxy
environment variables through; never require or hardcode a proxy.

## Engine and room contracts

Keep the engine deterministic and replayable. No I/O, clocks, concurrency,
logging or global randomness. Rules use integers only and no maps in rules logic;
use enum-indexed arrays. Draw all chance at game creation from the seed. If the
game needs later chance, record the entropy stream or outcomes for replay and
state that exception as an explicit decision. Clone independently.
[State the exact package import allow-list and enforce it with a test/linter.]

The server validates an action key by membership in the current engine legal
list and applies that engine-owned action. Never construct rules actions from
client payloads. Per-viewer views omit every hidden fact the viewer cannot
know, including spectators and logs. Distinguish public facts, private player
knowledge and server-only state. Test adversarial viewers and stale/replayed
requests. For a two-player game, the moderator set may freeze at Start: no promotions,
demotions, temporary moderators or seat switches during play; normal lobby
roles still apply before Start. Identity/device-link rules remain in full.
Record this simplification as an explicit decision. Room actors serialize state changes. Persist rules/preset versions
and deterministic entropy. Ignore late worker results after undo/restore.

Wire fields use explicit JSON names, empty arrays rather than null, and typed
codes/events rather than English prose. Keep optionality unambiguous. Write
rules tests from docs, not by copying implementation assumptions.

## Client, accessibility and tooltips

Svelte 5 runes and event properties only. Prefer derived values; avoid effects
that write state. Render whole server views with `$state.raw`. The client renders
legal choices and sends their keys; it computes no costs, rules or legality.
Keep leaf components usable in a standalone gallery. Use stable `data-act`
action keys and semantic fixture anchors for browser tests.

Phone-first layouts, no horizontal board scroll, logical CSS for direction.
Below the supported minimum size, show a notice asking for a larger window or
rotation. A tap dismisses it and exposes the squeezed layout anyway.
Translate all user-visible text and accessible names. User names use `dir="auto"`.
State must remain legible when animation is disabled. Launch with one deliberate
theme chosen with the owner. One selected theme pack controls all colours and
the CSS colour scheme. Add further packs later. A dark look is its own explicit
theme, which may inherit art; do not add automatic light/dark switching from
the OS. If the game ships one theme, expose no redundant theme selector.

Use the custom tooltip helper, never native `title=` attributes. Each glyph or
micro-element gets short translated meaning unless it repeats a visible label.
Use only server-supplied numbers. The innermost registered anchor wins. Fine
pointer hover waits briefly, keyboard focus shows it, Escape closes it. Passive
touch tap shows; actionable touch tap still acts, long-press shows and its
release never acts. A tap closing an open tip is swallowed. One tip at a time.
Keep icon-only controls accessibly named. Tooltips cannot be the only route to
information needed for a move: card sheets and rules must carry it too.

Linked previews are required: a tooltip may carry at most two caller-rendered
real-component previews in a joined second panel, beside on wide screens and
on the bubble's far side on phones. No nested stack. An optional stable
`rulesId` shows a translated More in Rules link, closes the tip and opens that
rules section. The rules link and deliberately marked `data-tooltip-interactive` bubble
controls take the pointer; preserve hover intent, keyboard access and their
touch-tap exception. Other bubble content lets board clicks pass. The complete rules reference is also required: a live-board
desktop side drawer, tall phone bottom sheet, and `/rules` page sharing the same
translated sections, stable ids and real component diagrams. Home links the
page and the game menu opens the overlay. Replace skeleton rule text before
shipping; never treat placeholder translation keys as completed rules.

Construct WebSocket URLs from the page origin with ws/wss. Plain HTTP and
intranets must work, including clipboard fallback. Never use remote fonts,
assets or captcha. Keep identity tokens out of URLs, logs and screenshots.

## Assets and documentation

Hand-author vectors through render, inspect and revise loops. Check final
batches in a browser. Generate bitmaps one at a time in the codex lane. Keep
approved pixel-identical lossless WebP originals, derive shipped AVIF with byte
budgets, and require an explicit review of the exact attempt. Rejected attempts
stay private. Use the per-theme index with content-hashed URLs; missing art is
a procedural placeholder. No copied official art, logos or frame designs.
Keep painting quality rules in the theme's style file. Self-host licensed fonts.

Update docs in the same commit as the behavior they describe. Keep README
short: value, quick start and links to details. `docs/testing.md` says how to
run each check and what it proves; acceptance evidence goes in the one file
`docs/dev/acceptance.md`, updated in place, never as a separate commit that only
records acceptance. No tables in prose docs. Record provenance and asset
licences. Keep private state, run logs, generated review sheets,
service targets, credentials, hostnames and personal data out of public commits.

## Commits, deployment and unattended work

Stay on the assigned branch/worktree. Touch only owned paths. Preserve other
sessions' changes. Split cohesive changes into atomic commits. Check status
immediately before committing, commit with explicit pathspecs, then inspect
`git show --stat`. Include the actual authoring model in the required trailer.
Do not rewrite published history. A worker does not push unless its brief
assigns that duty. Report signing failures and use unsigned commits if needed.

After finishing and committing, update the local/dev instance inside tmux
session `<repo-name>-setup`, wait 120 seconds, then read its logs: `dev-start`
for local development, `redeploy` for a local built instance. Production
redeployment follows the owner's deployment decision `<D-number>` in
`docs/dev/decisions.md` (who may redeploy, after which checks); without such a
decision there is no deployment authority, and the post-commit rule grants
none. Check the remembered URL before updating.
An isolated worker reports deployment needs to the coordinator. Never claim a
serving URL until startup checks pass.

A pending permission or policy question blocks only actions that depend on it.
Contain an incident's active exposure first, preserve evidence without copying
secrets, then continue independent authorized work. Do not rotate unrelated
credentials without authority. Before reporting idle, select an unblocked task
or name the external dependency for every remaining task. Existing approvals
persist within their scope; do not repeat settled votes or unchanged checks.

Runs unattended: the brief settles routine design questions within its scope.
Read the private learnings index and recovery note first. Check whether the
slice is already committed. Save a result even after a failed check, with first
line DONE or PARTIAL, commits, check output, artifact paths/sizes, concerns and
single-writer requests. Record coordinator messages in the worker checkpoint;
the owner's verbatim file contains only the owner's words.

Owner phrases are literal steering commands, not agent-generated suggestions:

- "switch to nightshift [HH:MM-HH:MM]": set the guard's window and persist it;
  arrange approved wakeups and wind-down at its edges.
- "switch to continuous": clear the window schedule and set continuous mode.
- "pause <project>": wind down owned heavy jobs, then set paused mode.
- "resume <project>": restore the prior mode and resume from durable state.
- "<project> status": rebuild the report from commits, results and logs.

Maintain one private state directory with briefs, results, checkpoints and an
artifact retention ledger. Arm completion notification when launching a job;
do not spend model turns polling. On interruption, checkpoint before yielding.
